OK, but the you'll have to limit the effects of the cancellation wand
effects a bit - remember that some character classes rely on magic items...
How about making wand of cancellation affect only a finite number
of items?

> B) Have a "luck stealer" in the game (like gremlins). What with
> a guaranteed luck stone now, this makes for super-charaters later on.
> Gremlins or leprachauns (say) that stole your luck would make the game
> rather more difficult. 
>
Or have luck getting 'used up' - if your luck helped you in some
situation (like wand of death missed BECAUSE of your luck) - you loose one
luck point. This way your luck is finite andwears off if often used...

C) I agree that the "bigguns" should be more 
> impressive. Dragons are far too whimpy. Perhaps there should be older
> (and larger) dragons further down. How about Beholders (`a la AD&D) for
> really scary eye-guys?
> 
Be a bit careful. Remember what happens in gnomish mines when gnomes
start deliberately jumping into these polymorph traps? Right now in the
mines I can survive a single blast from most monsters and have at least
some chance to escape. For the sake of balance on lower levels - don't 
overdo it.

I think much of this 'unbalance' on high levels comes from the fact that
you know all the spoilers and can build a very powerful character.
Think what would happen if you were alone with nethack - how long would
it take to discover what artifacts are there, what is the best armor etc?
You build an overpowered character because you know how to do it - a normal
player (i.e. one without access to rec.games.hack) probably does not find
the game unbalanced at high levels at all... Not without reason spoilers
are called spoilers - they just spoiled the fun for you.

But I have to partially agree that high level characters are bit too powerful
- but instead of tougher monsters i propose weaker characters...Make
hit points grow more slowly, make intrinsic resistances only partially
effective (with fire resistance you get only a quarter of fire damage -
but you get it) - this will restore balance on high levels without 
unbalancing the game even further on low levels.

